p.src:
For a quantum-mechanical wave, the momentum is $p=\frac{h}{\lambda}
=\frac{2\pi\hbar}{\lambda}
=\hbar k.$ Thus $k$ and $p$ differ only by the factor $\hbar$. Positive and negative $k$ describe waves moving in opposite directions and particles with oppositely directed momentum. In units with $\hbar=1$, it is common to call $k$ the momentum without further distinction.

